Fillets: where patch 0870 walks a run of a stripe's pieces again and the
run holds the stripe's first or last piece, the stripe's curve at that end
gets its parameter curve on the new pieces' surface (mitcad#1858).

Expected: the edge where a closed stripe's blend closes on itself (the
stripe's first and last curve, one curve) lies on both blend faces beside
it within the walk's 3D tolerance, as every other edge between its pieces.
Actual: a stadium-shaped block 40 deep with a hole of radius 12 in its
middle, the hole's rim rounded with 6, so that the top is a strip 2 wide in
front of the hole and behind it; the top's outer rim, one closed chain of
tangent edges, rounded with 3. The contact on the top crosses the rounded
rim's tangent edge and comes back in front and behind, and 0870 walks
those runs again. Started from the front edge, the front run holds the
stripe's first pieces: the closing edge's tolerance is 4.33 mm. Started
from the right arc, the back run holds its last pieces: 174 mm, and the
solid is not valid. A reported nut design (a closed chain of 38 edges
rounded with 5, mitcad#1858) closed with a tolerance of 4.79 mm, which no
later step could repair.

Cause (traced): ChFi3d_MakeExtremities makes a periodic stripe's end
curve from the first piece's first section and its parameter curve on the
last piece's surface (LastPCurve), a line between that piece's contacts
at its end. KeepFacesAcrossTangentEdges runs after it in Compute and, for
a run ending at the last piece, replaces the pieces with three on a newly
walked surface (KeepFaceAcrossTangentEdge). That surface has its own
parameters (here the section's parameter runs to 28.7 where the old one
ran to 1.22), so the old line on it lies off the curve by up to the
section's length, and ChFi3d_FilDS puts the edge there. The stripe's
contract that its end curves lie on its end pieces' surfaces is broken by
the replacement, not by the end curves' construction.

- KeepFaceAcrossTangentEdge: where the run starts at the stripe's first
  piece or ends at its last, after the replacement the stripe's parameter
  curve at that end is made again on the new end piece's surface
  (endPCurveOnPiece), as ChFi3d_MakeExtremities makes it: the line between
  the piece's contacts at that end, from ChFi3d_ComputePCurv, which also
  gives the tolerance reached; the end curve's tolerance is raised to it.
  The 3D curve stays: the run is walked again between the same sections.
- Runs inside a stripe, and stripes with no curve at an end (a corner
  there makes its own), are unchanged.

Upstream: KeepFacesAcrossTangentEdges is Mitcad's (0870); stock OCCT has
no step that replaces a stripe's pieces after its extremities are made,
so the change does nothing there. Within the port, JoinOverNarrowFaceFrom
also takes pieces off a stripe's ends; its stripes end at corners, which
make their end curves afterwards.
Test: geometry/tests/port/p0871-closing-curve-after-a-kept-face.cpp (ctest
geometry.port.0871): the block above, the chain started from the front
edge and from the right arc: one valid solid, every tolerance within the
walk's 1e-4, the same volume; the boundary, a hole of radius 9 leaving the
strip 5 wide (no run), as before. Before the patch the first two fail
(4.33 and 174, the second not valid).
